</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10 (no augmentation). Late-epoch metrics for HRM and the CNN baseline under identical optimization settings and preprocessing. HRM overfits rapidly (near-1.0 train accuracy) yet attains only <math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65.04\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65.04</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65.04\%</annotation></semantics></math> test accuracy at epoch 25; the CNN reaches <math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.22\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>77.22</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.22\%</annotation></semantics></math> while training much faster per epoch.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under the same regime (no augmentation, identical optimizer family, label smoothing), this CNN baseline attains <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">77.22%</span> test accuracy and trains roughly <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster per epoch</span> than HRM, whereas HRM reaches only <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">65.04%</span> after <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> epochs despite near-saturated training accuracy (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.99</annotation></semantics></math> over epochs 21–25).As shown by the HRM loss curve and error grid (Figs. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 CIFAR-10 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 CIFAR-10 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the previous subsection), HRM optimizes smoothly but overfits quickly, while the CNN exhibits better generalization on CIFAR-10 in this setting (Figs. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2.1 CIFAR-10: CNN baseline ‣ 3.2 CIFAR-10 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2.1 CIFAR-10: CNN baseline ‣ 3.2 CIFAR-10 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).Overall, these results indicate that <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">with no augmentation and minimal regularization on small natural images</em>, a simple convolutional architecture is substantially more suitable than HRM for CIFAR-10 classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>CIFAR-100</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation of the HRM on CIFAR-100 follows the same no-augmentation image-classification protocol as CIFAR-10.
Inputs are per-channel standardized RGB images of shape <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32{\times}32{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32{\times}32{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Tokenization uses non-overlapping <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4{\times}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4{\times}4</annotation></semantics></math> patches, yielding a sequence length of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{+}8{\times}8{=}65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{+}8{\times}8{=}65</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (including a learned <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span> token).
Both the low- and high-level modules (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{L},f_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{L},f_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>) are encoder-only Transformer stacks with shared dimensionality: <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}{=}192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}{=}192</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{heads}}{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>heads</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{heads}}{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> (per-head dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>), MLP multiplier <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> layers in each of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Training uses the HRM recurrent schedule of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> high-level cycles and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> low-level updates per cycle, deep supervision with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{train}}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{train}}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> segments (state detached between segments), and the one-step gradient approximation (all but the final <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/H</annotation></semantics></math> updates run under <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stop_gradient</span>).
At evaluation time, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{eval}}{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{eval}}{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> segments are run with fresh initial states per batch.
Optimization is AdamW (global-norm clipping <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>), linear warmup for one epoch followed by cosine decay with a <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> floor; learning rate <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>, label smoothing <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon{=}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon{=}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, and no dropout or stochastic depth.
Training is run for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> epochs with the above settings and no adaptive computation/halting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03598v1/x5.svg" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/344;" width="476" height="344"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">HRM on CIFAR-100: training loss per step (light) with a moving-average smoothed trace.
The curve decreases smoothly without instabilities, indicating stable optimization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03598v1/x6.png" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/327;" width="476" height="327" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S3.F8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">HRM on CIFAR-100: examples misclassified by the final model.
Each tile shows the ground-truth (<span id="S3.F8.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T</span>) and prediction (<span id="S3.F8.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P</span>) indices.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The loss trace in Fig. <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.3 CIFAR-100 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows a steady, monotonic decrease across training, similar to the CIFAR-10 behavior, which confirms that the one-step gradient with deep supervision optimizes stably in this setting.
However, the model rapidly overfits on CIFAR-100: by epoch 25, the training accuracy reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="91.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>91.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">91.53\%</annotation></semantics></math> while the test accuracy is only <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29.70%</span>.
This widening train–test gap, together with the diverse misclassifications in Fig. <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 3.3 CIFAR-100 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, indicates that in the no-augmentation regime on a <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>-class natural-image task, HRM functions as a high-capacity learner without the inductive bias needed for good generalization.
Typical epoch throughput was <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>391 steps/epoch at <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1.11 it/s (about <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{:}52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{:}52</annotation></semantics></math> per epoch), so extending training beyond <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> epochs would primarily increase training accuracy and wall-clock time without addressing the lack of generalization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>CIFAR-100: CNN baseline</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the same small convolutional network as in the CIFAR-10 baseline: two Conv–BN–ReLU blocks with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math> kernels per stage followed by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{\times}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{\times}2</annotation></semantics></math> max pooling (spatial resolution <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32{\times}32\rightarrow 16{\times}16\rightarrow 8{\times}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32{\times}32\rightarrow 16{\times}16\rightarrow 8{\times}8</annotation></semantics></math>), then global average pooling and a linear classifier. The base width is <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> channels in the first stage and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> in the second; there is no dropout. Inputs are per-channel standardized RGB images (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32{\times}32{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32{\times}32{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>) with <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no data augmentation</em>. Optimization mirrors the HRM runs: AdamW with linear warmup (one epoch) followed by cosine decay with a <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> floor, global-norm clipping <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, label smoothing <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math> training epochs. The final CNN has <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="273{,}344" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>273,344</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">273{,}344</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. Typical epoch throughput is <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>391 steps at <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>31 it/s (about <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> s/epoch).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03598v1/x7.svg" id="S3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/342;" width="476" height="342"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S3.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CNN on CIFAR-100: training loss per step (light) with a moving-average smoothed trace (dark).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03598v1/x8.png" id="S3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/327;" width="476" height="327" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S3.F10.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CNN on CIFAR-100: examples misclassified by the final model. Each tile shows ground-truth (<span id="S3.F10.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T</span>) and prediction (<span id="S3.F10.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3.1 CIFAR-100: CNN baseline ‣ 3.3 CIFAR-100 ‣ 3 Results ‣ Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> aggregates the late-epoch metrics for the CNN alongside the HRM trained under the same <em id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">raw</em> regime (no augmentation, identical optimizer family, label smoothing). The CNN improves steadily to <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">45.28%</span> test accuracy by epoch 25 while maintaining moderate training accuracy (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.50</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas HRM reaches near-saturated training accuracy (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.92</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.92</annotation></semantics></math>) yet achieves only <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29.70%</span> on the test set. Combined with the per-epoch wall-time difference (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>12 s vs. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5:52), this indicates that, for CIFAR-100 without augmentation, a simple convolutional architecture generalizes substantially better and is much more compute-efficient than HRM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T3.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HRM</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CNN</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epoch</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train loss</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train acc</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test acc</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train loss</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train acc</span></th>
<th id="S3.T3.3.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.3.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test acc</span></th></tr>
</thead>
